module tecs.audio.synth

--[[
General MIDI from a SoundFont, rendered in-process as one voice of the mixer.

A [`SoundFont`](tecs.audio.synth.SoundFont) is loaded once per mixer and
shared by every [`Synth`](tecs.audio.synth.Synth) made on it. A synth takes one
voice slot the way `Audio.play` takes one, so it counts against `maxVoices`,
joins a group, and follows group gain, group mute, group pause, master gain and
master mute like any other voice. What it adds is a channel for MIDI: sixteen
General MIDI channels driven by short messages a game sends from the frame
thread.

```nupp
local mixer <const> = tecs.audio.newAudio()
local font <const> = assert(tecs.audio.synth.loadSoundFont(mixer, "assets/music/FluidR3_GM.sf2"))
local music <const> = assert(tecs.audio.synth.new(mixer, font, {group = "music"}))

-- A C major scale, scheduled ahead on the synthesizer clock.
local start <const> = music:now() + 0.1
music:programChange(0, 0)
for step, key in ipairs({60, 62, 64, 65, 67, 69, 71, 72}) do
    local at <const> = start + (step - 1) * 0.25
    music:noteOn(0, key, 100, at)
    music:noteOff(0, key, at + 0.22)
end
```

Values are MIDI's own. Channels count from zero to fifteen, as on the wire, so
the General MIDI percussion channel is 9. Keys, velocities, controllers,
controller values and programs run from zero to 127, and a pitch bend from zero
to 16383 with 8192 at the centre. A value outside its range raises: it is a
mistake in the call, and a byte silently masked into range plays a wrong note
rather than failing.

## The clock and `at`

Every sending method takes an optional `at`, in seconds on the synthesizer's
own clock, which `now` reads. The clock is the frames the synthesizer has
rendered divided by the output rate, so it is the audio clock rather than the
frame clock: it does not drift against what is heard, it never runs backwards,
and it stands still while nothing renders the synth, which is the case before
the first output buffer, while its group is paused, and for good on a mixer
with no output.

An event lands on the first point of the render grid at or after `at`. The
synthesizer applies MIDI between internal blocks of eight frames, so an event
is never early and at most seven frames late, which is 0.15 milliseconds at
48 kHz, and where it lands depends only on `at`. That last property is the one
to build on: the same schedule renders the same samples on every run and every
machine, whatever the frame rate did.

An omitted `at`, and one already in the past, means the next block, and events
landing on the same block apply in the order they were sent. Sending each event
as it falls due therefore works, but it puts every note on whichever block the
next output buffer starts with, so its timing follows the frame rate. A
sequencer sends a little ahead instead, against `now`: a lookahead of one or
two frames' worth, say 50 milliseconds, absorbs an ordinary hitch and keeps the
timing exact.

## The queue

Events cross to the audio thread through a bounded queue the output callback
never waits on, and that thread holds as many again in its schedule for events
sent ahead of their time. Either is 4096 events. A send that finds no room is
refused rather than waiting or growing anything: the method returns false and
the event is gone. A game streaming a song with a short lookahead never comes
near the bound. One that sends a whole song up front can, so schedule a window
at a time rather than everything at once.

## Without an output

A mixer with no output device, or one made with a test backend, still makes a
synth whose every call succeeds. On a mixer with no output nothing renders, so
nothing is heard and `now` stays at zero. A test backend that supplies
`openSynth` receives every event the game sends through the
[`SynthPort`](tecs.platform.audiobackend.SynthPort) it returns, which is how a
harness logs them; one that does not gets the silent port, which accepts and
drops them. A backend without `loadSoundFont` loads nothing, and its SoundFont
is a name.

## Hardware MIDI output

Rendering in-process is the only output for now. A hardware output, through
CoreMIDI, WinMM or ALSA, would be a second implementation of `SynthPort` with
the same `send` and `at` contract, chosen when the synth is made. Nothing above
the port would change.
]]

local audio = require("tecs.audio")
local audiobackend = require("tecs.platform.audiobackend")
local audiosynth = require("tecs.internal.audiosynth")

--- Notes a synthesizer sounds at once unless asked otherwise.
local DEFAULT_POLYPHONY: integer = 64

--- The range the synthesizer accepts for its polyphony.
local MIN_POLYPHONY: integer = 8

local MAX_POLYPHONY: integer = 256

--- The channel `notesOff` reads as every channel.
local ALL_CHANNELS: integer = -1

--- A loaded SoundFont, shared by every synth made on it.
export record SoundFont
    --- Read-only. Reports the path the SoundFont was loaded from, exactly as
    --- given. It is the SoundFont's identity within its mixer, so loading the
    --- same path again returns this record.
    path: string

    @private mixer: audio.Audio

    --- The backend's id for it, and zero for a backend that holds none.
    @private id: integer
end

--- Configures `new`.
---
--- Read once, when the synth is made. Nothing here is retained.
export type Options = {
    --- Caller-writable. Selects the group the synth's voice joins, so the
    --- group's gain, mute and pause apply to it. Defaults to none.
    group: string?,

    --- Caller-writable. Sets the voice's linear gain before group and master
    --- gain, and defaults to one.
    gain: number?,

    --- Caller-writable. Sets how many notes sound at once, from 8 to 256, and
    --- defaults to 64. Past it the synthesizer takes the oldest note for a new
    --- one.
    polyphony: integer?,

    --- Caller-writable. Turns the synthesizer's reverb and chorus on, and
    --- defaults to true. Off, a released note stops at the end of its own
    --- release rather than ringing on in the reverb.
    effects: boolean?
}

--- One General MIDI synthesizer, playing as one voice of a mixer.
---
--- Every sending method returns whether the event was queued, which is false
--- only when the bounded queue is full or the synth has been destroyed. None of
--- them raises for either, because a note that cannot be queued is not a
--- reason for a frame to stop. Nothing here is thread safe: call it from the
--- thread that owns the mixer.
export record Synth
    @private mixer: audio.Audio
    @private port: audiobackend.SynthPort
    @private voice: integer
    @private destroyed: boolean

    send: function(exclusive self: Synth, status: integer, data1: integer, data2: integer, at: number?): boolean
    noteOn: function(exclusive self: Synth, channel: integer, key: integer, velocity: integer, at: number?): boolean
    noteOff: function(exclusive self: Synth, channel: integer, key: integer, at: number?): boolean
    controlChange: function(
        exclusive self: Synth,
        channel: integer,
        controller: integer,
        value: integer,
        at: number?
    ): boolean
    programChange: function(exclusive self: Synth, channel: integer, program: integer, at: number?): boolean
    pitchBend: function(exclusive self: Synth, channel: integer, value: integer, at: number?): boolean
    allNotesOff: function(exclusive self: Synth, channel: integer?, immediate: boolean?): boolean
    reset: function(exclusive self: Synth): boolean
    now: function(borrows self: Synth): number
    setGain: function(exclusive self: Synth, gain: number): nil
    pause: function(exclusive self: Synth): nil
    resume: function(exclusive self: Synth): nil
    paused: function(borrows self: Synth): boolean
    destroy: function(exclusive self: Synth, fadeOut: number?): nil
end

--- The SoundFonts each mixer has loaded, by path, keyed weakly so a mixer that
--- has gone takes its fonts with it.
local loaded: {[audio.Audio]: {[string]: SoundFont}} = setmetatable({}, {__mode = "k"})

--- Returns what `tecs.audio` lent this module for one mixer.
local function lender(mixer: audio.Audio): audiosynth.Lender
    return assert(audiosynth.lent[mixer], "tecs.audio registered no synthesizer seam for this mixer")
end

--- Raises unless `value` is from `low` to `high`.
--- @raises string when the value is outside the range
local function check(name: string, what: string, value: integer, low: integer, high: integer): nil
    if value < low or value > high then
        error(
            "tecs.audio.synth." .. name .. ": " .. what .. " must be from " .. tostring(
                low
            ) .. " to " .. tostring(high) .. ", got " .. tostring(value),
            3
        )
    end
end

--- Loads a SoundFont 2 file into a mixer and returns it.
---
--- Loading the same path into the same mixer again returns the same record, so
--- every synth on it shares one copy of its samples. A General MIDI set is
--- large, FluidR3 GM is 140 megabytes, and the load reads all of it before it
--- returns, so do it while loading rather than while playing.
---
--- Only load a SoundFont the game ships. The reader checks the file is a
--- SoundFont 2, but it is not hardened against a crafted one.
---
--- @param mixer the mixer whose synths will use it
--- @param path the file, which is also the SoundFont's identity in this mixer
--- @return the SoundFont, or nil when the file could not be read
--- @return the reason, when the first return is nil
--- @raises string when the path is empty
export function loadSoundFont(mixer: audio.Audio, path: string): (SoundFont?, string?)
    if path == "" then
        error("tecs.audio.synth.loadSoundFont: a SoundFont path cannot be empty", 2)
    end
    local fonts = loaded[mixer]
    if fonts == nil then
        fonts = {}
        loaded[mixer] = fonts
    end
    local existing = fonts[path]
    if existing ~= nil then
        return existing
    end

    local load = lender(mixer).backend().loadSoundFont
    local id: integer = 0
    if load ~= nil then
        local font, reason = load(path)
        if font == nil then
            return nil, reason or ("cannot load " .. path)
        end
        id = font
    end
    local font = new SoundFont(path = path, mixer = mixer, id = id)
    fonts[path] = font

    return font
end

--- Makes a synthesizer on a SoundFont and starts its voice.
---
--- The voice starts sounding with the mixer's next flush, which `update` makes
--- once per frame, and events sent before then wait for it. It sounds until
--- `destroy`, and a voice `stopAll` or `stopGroup` ended does not come back:
--- make another synth instead.
---
--- @param mixer the mixer the voice plays on, which must be the one the
---     SoundFont was loaded into
--- @param font the SoundFont to play
--- @param options the group, gain, polyphony and effects, or nil for the
---     defaults
--- @return the synth, or nil when its voice could not start. A mixer with no
---     output is not such a case: it returns a synth that renders nothing.
--- @return the reason, when the first return is nil: every voice is busy, or
---     the backend refused the synthesizer
--- @raises string when the SoundFont belongs to another mixer
--- @raises string when `polyphony` is outside 8 to 256
export function new(mixer: audio.Audio, font: SoundFont, options: Options?): (Synth?, string?)
    if font.mixer ~= mixer then
        error("tecs.audio.synth.new: the SoundFont was loaded into another mixer", 2)
    end
    local polyphony = (options and options.polyphony) or DEFAULT_POLYPHONY
    if polyphony < MIN_POLYPHONY or polyphony > MAX_POLYPHONY then
        error("tecs.audio.synth.new: polyphony must be from 8 to 256, got " .. tostring(polyphony), 2)
    end
    local effects = not (options ~= nil and options.effects == false)

    local seam = lender(mixer)
    local voice = seam.start((options and options.gain) or 1, options and options.group)
    if voice == 0 then
        if mixer.available then
            return nil, "every voice is busy, so the synthesizer has none to play through"
        end

        return new Synth(mixer = mixer, port = audiobackend.silentSynth(), voice = 0, destroyed = false)
    end

    local open = seam.backend().openSynth
    if open == nil or font.id == 0 then
        return new Synth(mixer = mixer, port = audiobackend.silentSynth(), voice = voice, destroyed = false)
    end
    -- Opened before the voice's start is flushed, because the start is what
    -- hands the synthesizer to the mixer.
    local port, reason = open(font.id, voice, polyphony, effects)
    if port == nil then
        mixer:stop(voice)

        return nil, reason or "the backend opened no synthesizer"
    end

    return new Synth(mixer = mixer, port = port, voice = voice, destroyed = false)
end

--- Queues any short MIDI message.
---
--- @param self the synth to send to
--- @param status the status byte, from 0x80 to 0xFF. A channel message
---     carries its channel in the low four bits. System messages, 0xF0 and up,
---     are accepted and have no effect on this synthesizer.
--- @param data1 the first data byte, from 0 to 127
--- @param data2 the second data byte, from 0 to 127, and 0 for a message that
---     carries one
--- @param at seconds on the synthesizer clock, where nil or a time already
---     passed means the next block
--- @return whether the event was queued, false when the queue is full or the
---     synth is destroyed
--- @raises string when a byte is outside its range
function Synth.send(exclusive self: Synth, status: integer, data1: integer, data2: integer, at: number?): boolean
    check("send", "status", status, 0x80, 0xFF)
    check("send", "data1", data1, 0, 127)
    check("send", "data2", data2, 0, 127)
    if self.destroyed then
        return false
    end

    return self.port.send(status, data1, data2, at or 0)
end

--- Queues a note on.
---
--- @param self the synth to send to
--- @param channel from 0 to 15, where 9 is General MIDI percussion
--- @param key from 0 to 127, where 60 is middle C
--- @param velocity from 0 to 127. Zero is a note off, as MIDI defines it.
--- @param at seconds on the synthesizer clock, read as `send` reads it
--- @return whether the event was queued
--- @raises string when a value is outside its range
function Synth.noteOn(exclusive self: Synth, channel: integer, key: integer, velocity: integer, at: number?): boolean
    check("noteOn", "channel", channel, 0, 15)
    check("noteOn", "key", key, 0, 127)
    check("noteOn", "velocity", velocity, 0, 127)

    return self:send(0x90 + channel, key, velocity, at)
end

--- Queues a note off, which lets the note play its release.
---
--- @param self the synth to send to
--- @param channel from 0 to 15
--- @param key from 0 to 127
--- @param at seconds on the synthesizer clock, read as `send` reads it
--- @return whether the event was queued
--- @raises string when a value is outside its range
function Synth.noteOff(exclusive self: Synth, channel: integer, key: integer, at: number?): boolean
    check("noteOff", "channel", channel, 0, 15)
    check("noteOff", "key", key, 0, 127)

    return self:send(0x80 + channel, key, 0, at)
end

--- Queues a control change.
---
--- @param self the synth to send to
--- @param channel from 0 to 15
--- @param controller from 0 to 127, such as 7 for channel volume, 10 for pan,
---     64 for the sustain pedal and 0 for bank select
--- @param value from 0 to 127
--- @param at seconds on the synthesizer clock, read as `send` reads it
--- @return whether the event was queued
--- @raises string when a value is outside its range
function Synth.controlChange(
    exclusive self: Synth,
    channel: integer,
    controller: integer,
    value: integer,
    at: number?
): boolean
    check("controlChange", "channel", channel, 0, 15)
    check("controlChange", "controller", controller, 0, 127)
    check("controlChange", "value", value, 0, 127)

    return self:send(0xB0 + channel, controller, value, at)
end

--- Queues a program change, selecting an instrument for later notes.
---
--- @param self the synth to send to
--- @param channel from 0 to 15
--- @param program from 0 to 127, the General MIDI instrument numbered from
---     zero, so 0 is the acoustic grand piano
--- @param at seconds on the synthesizer clock, read as `send` reads it
--- @return whether the event was queued
--- @raises string when a value is outside its range
function Synth.programChange(exclusive self: Synth, channel: integer, program: integer, at: number?): boolean
    check("programChange", "channel", channel, 0, 15)
    check("programChange", "program", program, 0, 127)

    return self:send(0xC0 + channel, program, 0, at)
end

--- Queues a pitch bend.
---
--- @param self the synth to send to
--- @param channel from 0 to 15
--- @param value the 14-bit bend from 0 to 16383, where 8192 is no bend. The
---     range it spans is the channel's, two semitones each way by default.
--- @param at seconds on the synthesizer clock, read as `send` reads it
--- @return whether the event was queued
--- @raises string when a value is outside its range
function Synth.pitchBend(exclusive self: Synth, channel: integer, value: integer, at: number?): boolean
    check("pitchBend", "channel", channel, 0, 15)
    check("pitchBend", "value", value, 0, 16383)

    return self:send(0xE0 + channel, value % 128, value // 128, at)
end

--- Releases every sounding note, for the next block.
---
--- Takes no `at`: it is what a game sends to stop, now. Notes scheduled for
--- later still play when their time comes; `reset` is the call that discards
--- them too.
---
--- @param self the synth to send to
--- @param channel from 0 to 15, or nil for every channel
--- @param immediate true cuts the notes without their release, and nil or
---     false lets each one fade as a note off would
--- @return whether the event was queued
--- @raises string when the channel is outside its range
function Synth.allNotesOff(exclusive self: Synth, channel: integer?, immediate: boolean?): boolean
    if channel ~= nil then
        check("allNotesOff", "channel", channel, 0, 15)
    end
    if self.destroyed then
        return false
    end

    return self.port.notesOff(channel or ALL_CHANNELS, immediate == true)
end

--- Returns every channel to its General MIDI defaults, for the next block.
---
--- Cuts every note, puts every channel back on program zero with its
--- controllers at rest, and discards every event sent before this call that
--- has not been applied, scheduled ones included. Events sent after it apply
--- as usual.
---
--- @param self the synth to reset
--- @return whether the reset was queued
function Synth.reset(exclusive self: Synth): boolean
    if self.destroyed then
        return false
    end

    return self.port.reset()
end

--- Returns the synthesizer clock, for computing `at`.
---
--- @param self the synth to read
--- @return seconds of audio the synthesizer has rendered: monotonic, zero
---     before its first block, still while its group is paused, and zero for
---     good on a mixer with no output. It leads what is heard by the output's
---     own latency, and leads the mix by at most one block.
function Synth.now(borrows self: Synth): number
    if self.destroyed then
        return 0
    end

    return self.port.now()
end

--- Sets the synth's gain, before its group's and the master's.
---
--- @param self the synth to scale
--- @param gain linear amplitude rather than decibels, applied from the next
---     output buffer rather than ramped
function Synth.setGain(exclusive self: Synth, gain: number): nil
    self.mixer:setGain(self.voice, gain)
end

--- Pauses the synth's voice, which stops its clock with it.
---
--- Nothing is lost: every note holds where it stood, events already scheduled
--- wait on the stopped clock, and `resume` carries on exactly where it left
--- off.
---
--- @param self the synth to pause
function Synth.pause(exclusive self: Synth): nil
    self.mixer:pause(self.voice)
end

--- Resumes a paused synth.
---
--- @param self the synth to resume
function Synth.resume(exclusive self: Synth): nil
    self.mixer:resume(self.voice)
end

--- Reports whether the synth's own voice is paused, leaving its group aside.
---
--- @param self the synth to inspect
--- @return true between `pause` and `resume`
function Synth.paused(borrows self: Synth): boolean
    return self.mixer:paused(self.voice)
end

--- Stops the synth's voice and frees its synthesizer. Safe more than once.
---
--- Without a fade the voice stops at once, so a note still sounding is cut;
--- send `allNotesOff` a little before for a softer ending. With one it fades
--- over `fadeOut` seconds, the synthesizer rendering on and taking no more
--- events, and goes when the fade ends.
---
--- @param self the synth to destroy
--- @param fadeOut seconds to fade over, or nil to stop at once
function Synth.destroy(exclusive self: Synth, fadeOut: number?): nil
    if self.destroyed then
        return
    end
    self.destroyed = true
    self.mixer:stop(self.voice, fadeOut)
    self.port.close()
end
